<TextPage
	title="Pattern terms — what you may do with these patterns"
	description="Every pattern here is free. Make them, gift them, sell what you make. Don't resell or republish the pattern itself."
	heading="Pattern terms"
	standfirst="Every pattern on this site is free to use. The short version: the things you make are yours, the pattern stays mine."
	updated="Last updated 19 August 2026"
>
	<h2>What you may do</h2>

	<ul>
		<li>Make as many items as you like from any pattern here.</li>
		<li>Make them for yourself, or as gifts.</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Sell what you make.</strong> Market stalls, craft fairs, your own shop — no
			permission needed and no licence to buy. You do not owe me a cut.
		</li>
		<li>Change the pattern to fit you. Resize it, restyle it, swap the yarn.</li>
		<li>Print a copy for your own use, and write on it.</li>
	</ul>

	<p>
		A credit and a link back are appreciated when you sell or post a finished piece, but they
		are a courtesy, not a condition.
	</p>

	<h2>What you may not do</h2>

	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Sell, republish or redistribute the pattern itself</strong> — the written
			instructions, in any form, on any platform, in any language.
		</li>
		<li>Copy the text into your own pattern, blog post or video description.</li>
		<li>Claim the pattern as your own design.</li>
		<li>Use my photographs anywhere without asking.</li>
		<li>
			Put the pattern behind a paywall, bundle it into something you sell, or upload it to a
			pattern-sharing site.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<p>
		If you want to share a pattern with someone, link to the page rather than copying the
		text. That way they get any corrections I make later.
	</p>

	<h2>Why it is phrased this way</h2>

	<p>
		Plenty of patterns carry a line forbidding the sale of finished items. I have deliberately
		not done that, for two reasons.
	</p>

	<p>
		The first is that I do not want to. If someone makes a cardigan from a free pattern and
		sells it at a market, that is a good outcome.
	</p>

	<p>
		The second is that such a restriction would be shaky in any case. Copyright protects the
		written pattern as a text. It does not generally extend to useful articles — a jumper, a
		blanket, a bag — made from it. And because these patterns are free rather than bought,
		there is no purchase and so no contract in which you could have agreed to such a term.
		A restriction I could not enforce, on something I do not object to, is just noise.
	</p>

	<p>
		What copyright does cover, clearly, is the pattern text and the photographs. Those are the
		lines above, and those I do mean.
	</p>

	<h2>Translations and teaching</h2>

	<p>
		Happy to be asked about both. Teaching a pattern in a class or a group is fine — send
		people to the page rather than handing out copies. For translations, get in touch first, so
		the translation can live here alongside the original rather than as a copy elsewhere.
	</p>

	<h2>Errors</h2>

	<p>
		If you find a mistake, please tell me. Patterns get corrected in place, and the page always
		holds the current version — another reason to link rather than copy.
	</p>

